WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.530
This video was made possible by CuriosityStream.

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:07.700
Watch an exclusive companion video to this
on Nebula, which you can access by signing

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:15.580
up for the CuriosityStream/Nebula bundle deal
for $15 a year at CuriosityStream.com/Wendover.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:20.600
With any disruptive technology, there’s
a tipping point—there is a point in time

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.330
when its path towards market dominance is
a certainty.

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:29.449
Now, electric vehicles are almost certainly
a disruptive technology—they’re almost

00:00:29.449 --> 00:00:34.170
certainly a technology that will, with time,
become dominant over their predecessor.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:40.269
In this case, the predecessor is the internal
combustion car that you, yourself almost certainly

00:00:40.269 --> 00:00:41.289
use.

00:00:41.289 --> 00:00:46.479
Chances are, though, when asked, you’d say
that your next car will not be electric, and

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:51.339
you’re right—the average consumer, according
to surveys, would not even consider purchasing

00:00:51.339 --> 00:00:56.079
an electric vehicle, demonstrating that the
technology is not yet at that tipping point

00:00:56.079 --> 00:00:58.889
where its on a certain path towards market
dominance.

00:00:58.889 --> 00:01:03.239
But again, that path is almost certain.

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:08.720
EVs are not there yet—right now, they’re
too expensive, too short range, and too slow

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.250
to charge—but they’re close.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:15.070
In fact, research can quantify just how close
they are.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:19.220
It’s been shown that the “tipping point
price” for EVs, the price that will lead

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:25.180
to mainstream adoption and eventual disruption,
is $36,000.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:29.680
Taking a look at the prices of the base-models
of three of the world’s best selling electric

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.220
vehicles, they’re already roughly there,
so we know that that’s not what’s holding

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.650
mass-market consumers back.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:39.750
What also matters is range.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:46.700
Consumers say they need 291 miles or 469 kilometers
of it before the cars can go mass-market.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:53.450
Two of those best-selling EVs, the Tesla Model
3 and Chevy Volt EV, are not far from that,

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:56.060
while the Nissan LEAF lags behind.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:00.130
Range and cost are closely linked, and you
can essentially trade one off for the other,

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:03.590
as the battery is the single largest cost
of an EV.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:08.819
That’s why the industry is so focused on
innovating and scaling to lower the component

00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:12.500
cost of EV batteries, and it’s working.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:19.719
In 2013, the average price per kWh of an EV
battery was $668, meaning the base-model Tesla

00:02:19.719 --> 00:02:26.950
Model 3’s 50 kWh battery would cost $22,400—2/3rds
of what the vehicle sells for.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:33.480
Nowadays, the average price per kWh is all
the way down to $137, meaning that same battery

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:41.269
pack would cost just $6,850, and this price
per kWh is expected to lower to $100 by 2023.

00:02:41.269 --> 00:02:46.319
It’s getting more and more possible for
manufacturers to sell an EV for the magic

00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:50.579
$36,000 price with the magic 291 mile range.

00:02:50.579 --> 00:02:55.999
While EVs are not quite there yet, they’re
really not far, and will be there in the next

00:02:55.999 --> 00:02:57.319
few years.

00:02:57.319 --> 00:03:02.720
So, range is not what’s significantly holding
mass-market consumers back, and it won’t

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.150
be at all within a few years.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:08.079
What is, though, is charging.

00:03:08.079 --> 00:03:11.800
The research shows that consumers want to
be able to charge their cars from empty to

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:17.980
full in 31 minutes, and that’s the magic
number for mass-market adoption.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:23.909
With this, current $36,000 EVs just aren’t
yet there.

00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:28.499
The base model Chevy Volt EV can’t even
fast-charge, it doesn’t have the technology

00:03:28.499 --> 00:03:33.859
for it, and even the upgraded, more expensive
model that does allow for fast-charging can

00:03:33.859 --> 00:03:37.819
only get to 39% state of charge in 31 minutes.

00:03:37.819 --> 00:03:43.419
The Nissan LEAF does a little better, attaining
62% state of charge in 31 minutes, while the

00:03:43.419 --> 00:03:48.830
base-model Tesla Model 3 does the best, with
its ability to fill its battery to 83% in

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:53.430
the most ideal conditions using the fastest
models of Tesla Superchargers, but that would

00:03:53.430 --> 00:04:00.239
only give it 196 miles or 315 kilometers of
range, again in the most ideal conditions.

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:05.209
In colder weather, both that charging time
would be greater and that range would be less.

00:04:05.209 --> 00:04:10.079
So, it’s currently possible to get an EV
with just about what the mass-market requires

00:04:10.079 --> 00:04:16.890
for cost and range, but reaching that charging
time—that’s just a lot tougher.

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:21.920
What this research can lead us to conclude
is that the largest barrier right now to mass-market

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.910
EV adoption is, in fact, the charging problem.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:31.660
The tipping-point just will not happen without
widespread fast charging, but widespread fast

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:37.270
charging is just difficult because of the
very way our electric grid works.

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:44.150
You see, back in the 1880s, Thomas Edison,
with his direct current electric system, battled

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:48.440
it out with George Westinghouse, and his alternating
current system.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:53.911
As the names suggest, direct current electricity
flows consistently and unidirectionally, while

00:04:53.911 --> 00:04:58.570
alternating current oscillates in magnitude
and rapidly changes direction.

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:03.440
The exact details of how each works isn’t
that important in this context, but what is

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:08.350
is to know that, for a variety of reasons,
AC power won, it’s now the standard for

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:13.630
power grids, but there are certain technologies
that still need DC power.

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:20.090
The most widespread example of that is batteries—you
cannot charge a battery using AC power.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:25.260
That’s why you don’t plug your smartphone
directly into an outlet—you plug it into

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:31.680
a power brick that plugs into an outlet, and
that power brick is an AC to DC inverter.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.580
A standard iPhone charging inverter outputs
5 watts of electricity, which is plenty enough

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:40.320
to charge the phone’s 11 watt hour battery
in a few hours.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:48.640
A base-model Tesla Model 3, meanwhile, has
a 50 kilowatt hour battery—4,500 times larger.

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:55.560
Therefore, it needs a much higher wattage
power inverter to charge with any speed.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:58.830
It solves this in two ways.

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:04.640
Onboard that Model 3, there’s a 7.7 kW inverter
that can take AC power from common sources,

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:09.380
like a standard wall outlet, and convert it
into DC power to charge the battery.

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:14.200
At its max rate, this can charge the car fully
in under ten hours, and has the advantage

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:18.850
of allowing consumers to charge using regular
wall plugs or by installing relatively inexpensive

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:22.520
chargers on existing domestic AC electric
circuits.

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:29.060
The disadvantage, though, is that, while 7.7
kW is plenty fast enough for regular, overnight,

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:33.410
at-home charging, it’s not fast enough to
compete with the convenience of filling up

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:36.130
an internal combustion car at the gas station.

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:40.700
It’s not fast enough if you’re on a long-distance
trip and need to be able to gain hundreds

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:43.910
of miles of range in a matter of minutes.

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:50.570
So, if you need more electricity faster, you
need a higher wattage inverter.

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:55.790
To be able to take a Tesla Model 3 from almost
empty to almost full in thirty minutes, you

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:59.210
want between 120 and 250 kW.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:07.880
The problem, though, is that a 250 kW inverter
costs, at least in this case, $57,600 and

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:14.420
is about the size of a very large fridge—it’s
not exactly practical to have this as an internal

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:16.160
component of the car.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:21.940
So, for faster charging, one needs to offboard
the inversion process.

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:29.540
That’s exactly what a DC-fast charger does—it
supplies a huge quantity of DC power to the

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:34.550
car, which bypasses the onboard inverter and
charges the battery directly.

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:38.780
Between the inverter, the charger, and all
the other equipment needed for a fast-charging

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:43.190
station, the cost and size is not insignificant.

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:48.170
One of the more popular models, the Chargepoint
Express 250, which can charge a single car

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:55.820
at a somewhat slow 62.5 kW, sells for $40,800,
and that’s before installation.

00:07:55.820 --> 00:08:01.620
Meanwhile, while it’s tough to get an exact
figure, industry experts estimate it costs

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:09.420
Tesla about $250,000 to build an average Supercharging
station with 6-8 stalls delivering 120 to

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:15.730
150 kW each, while its closest equivalent,
the stations by Volkswagen’s Electrify America,

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:19.530
are estimated to cost $350,000.

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:29.040
But here’s something counterintuitive: using
a 250 kW charger versus a 150 kW one doesn’t

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:32.000
really impact how fast you charge.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:37.779
Batteries charge slower the more full they
are, so the first 20% will pass far faster

00:08:37.779 --> 00:08:39.460
than the last 20%.

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:45.240
In the context of EV charging, this means
that quite quickly into the charge, the speed

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:50.380
is impacted not by how much power the station
is putting out, but by how much electricity

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:52.030
the battery can accept.

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:58.291
So, it’s actually faster to charge to 50%,
drive until empty, charge to 50%, and drive

00:08:58.291 --> 00:09:02.820
until empty again than charging to 100% and
driving to empty.

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:10.570
A Tesla Model 3 can go from zero to 50% charge
in 15 minutes on a 250 kW charger, and 17

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:17.210
minutes on a 150 kW charger—giving it enough
range to drive at least 100 miles or 160 kilometers—while

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:23.230
charging from 50% to 90% would take an additional
27 minutes in both cases.

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:29.230
So, combining two charges from empty to 50%,
in two stops, you could effectively reach

00:09:29.230 --> 00:09:36.640
the tipping point speed of 100% charge in
31 minutes with existing 250 kW chargers.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:43.170
Therefore, what the industry needs is not
faster chargers, but more chargers, which

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:48.210
is hugely difficult given the enormous cost
of fast chargers.

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:52.570
The average American lives four minutes away
from a gas station.

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:59.030
Meanwhile, the same average American lives
31 minutes away from their nearest Tesla Supercharger.

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:05.210
Currently, there are 976 supercharging stations
in the US—each of which have anywhere between

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:08.320
two and 56 individual chargers.

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:13.010
In order to match the four-minute average
of gas stations, Tesla would need to build

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:19.460
an additional 31,251 Supercharging stations.

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:26.710
At their $250,000 per station cost, that would
cost the company some $7.8 billion, or roughly

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:30.070
ten times their total annual profits from
2020.

00:10:30.070 --> 00:10:36.690
In addition, only some 750,000 Teslas ever
have been sold in the US, meaning, to have

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:41.520
fast charging stations as accessible as gas
stations, the company would need to install

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:48.000
a $250,000 Supercharging station for every
23 cars it had on the road.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:52.370
Quite obviously, that’s not feasible, as
the stations would never break-even with such

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:56.500
infrequent use, and that’s the exact problem.

00:10:56.500 --> 00:11:01.170
You need the infrastructure to sell the cars
but you can’t build the infrastructure until

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:02.170
you sell the cars.

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:05.860
It is the classic chicken and the egg problem.

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:09.430
There might, however, be a solution.

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:16.150
According to federal government data, there
are some 3,845 non-Tesla DC fast-chargers

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:21.600
in the US—the vast majority of which could
charge a Model 3 within an hour… assuming

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:23.029
it could connect.

00:11:23.029 --> 00:11:29.210
Just as there was a format war in the 1880s
between DC and AC power, there is now a war

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:31.070
of charging standards.

00:11:31.070 --> 00:11:36.550
Take the example of Salina, Kansas—a small
city off of Interstate 70 which most people

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:40.600
only visit to refuel or, in this case, recharge.

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:45.780
This Supercharger uses Tesla’s proprietary
plug, this Electrify America station uses

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:51.950
CCS and CHAdeMO plugs, and this hotel’s
charger uses a J-1772 plug.

00:11:51.950 --> 00:11:55.910
There are four different plug types in one
small city.

00:11:55.910 --> 00:12:01.779
Now, a Tesla could use the Tesla charger and
the J-1772 charger with an included adapter,

00:12:01.779 --> 00:12:07.120
but it could only use the CHAdeMO charger
with a speed-limited $540 adapter, and it

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:10.940
couldn’t use the CCS charger at all—as
there’s no adapter for that plug-type.

00:12:10.940 --> 00:12:16.980
Meanwhile, a Chevy Volt EV wouldn’t be able
to use the Tesla or CHAdeMO chargers at all

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:20.620
as there are no adapters available for either
to its CCS plug.

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:26.100
This means that, to accommodate every vehicle
type, DC fast chargers need to have three

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:31.279
different plug types, which, overwhelmingly,
they just don’t.

00:12:31.279 --> 00:12:35.690
Especially along Interstate highways, there
are the Tesla stations, and there are combo

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:38.440
CHAdeMO and CCS stations.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:42.490
Just like Edison and Westinghouse delayed
more widespread adoption of electric power

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:47.899
by competing against each other in the same
areas with their different, incompatible AC

00:12:47.899 --> 00:12:52.120
and DC standards, different stakeholders in
the electric vehicle market are competing

00:12:52.120 --> 00:12:57.320
against each other in the US to create redundant,
largely incompatible networks.

00:12:57.320 --> 00:13:00.260
But that’s not happening everywhere.

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:04.820
You see, in Europe, CCS is the standard.

00:13:04.820 --> 00:13:10.360
The European Union has a directive which means
that many member states, by law, require that

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:14.620
public DC fast chargers include a CCS plug.

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:20.290
Therefore, in the EU and neighboring countries
like the UK, Norway, and Switzerland, CCS

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:23.980
is now the de facto or de jure charging standard.

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:29.320
That forced Tesla’s hand to the point that
in 2018, it retrofitted all its existing Superchargers

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:34.839
with CCS plugs, switched its Model 3’s to
CCS, and released an adapter allowing its

00:13:34.839 --> 00:13:37.670
other models to use CCS chargers.

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:43.620
All told, this means that pretty much any
car in Europe can use pretty much any DC fast

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:44.620
charger.

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:49.149
That, combined with Europe’s higher population
density, has helped ensure that the density

00:13:49.149 --> 00:13:55.930
and coverage of DC fast chargers is much greater
than in the US, despite the fact that EV ownership

00:13:55.930 --> 00:14:00.650
per capita is actually higher in the US than
Europe as a whole—although certain European

00:14:00.650 --> 00:14:03.340
countries far eclipse the US’ rate.

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:07.640
Europe is almost identical in size to the
US, it has a very similar number of electric

00:14:07.640 --> 00:14:13.330
cars overall, but it has double the number
of DC-fast charging stations.

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:20.470
In Germany, the furthest you can seemingly
get from a DC-fast charger is here, in Winterberg.

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:25.630
From this small ski-town, the nearest fast
charger is about 30 miles or 50 kilometers

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:27.240
away in Marburg.

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:32.900
Meanwhile, in the US, if you wanted to drive
directly from Dallas to Denver, two major

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:37.080
cities, using a base-model Tesla Model 3,
you just couldn’t.

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:44.700
There’s a 226 mile or 363 kilometer stretch
with no DC fast charger between Amarillo,

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:50.390
Texas and Trinidad, Colorado which, given
the elevation gain, the car would not make.

00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:55.560
While Tesla is plugging this gap soon with
a new charger in Clayton, New Mexico, that

00:14:55.560 --> 00:15:00.270
won’t solve the problem for every single
other EV on the market, since the charging

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:02.800
systems are not compatible.

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:08.410
Simply put, mass market consumers are not
going to buy cars that can’t drive from

00:15:08.410 --> 00:15:10.480
Dallas to Denver.

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:16.030
What Europe has that the US does not is coordinated
government plans.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:21.089
Germany's federal government, for example,
builds its own charging stations, in addition

00:15:21.089 --> 00:15:25.210
to offering strong incentives for private
companies to do so as well.

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:30.080
Meanwhile, the federal government in the US
has done very little to incentivize fast-charger

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:34.200
construction, and certainly does not have
a network of its own.

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:39.190
Certain states, such as Oklahoma or Colorado,
do have strong, coordinated government programs

00:15:39.190 --> 00:15:44.050
to build fast charging infrastructure, meaning
even shorter-range EVs can drive essentially

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:48.350
anywhere in each state without encountering
a fast-charger gap, but the problem is that

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:53.519
EV drivers from Colorado or Oklahoma will
eventually want to drive through Kansas, or

00:15:53.519 --> 00:15:58.940
Nebraska, or Wyoming, or other states that
do not have a coordinated plan.

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:04.800
The US Federal Government clearly wants people
to buy EVs, because it offers hefty tax credits

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:10.410
to those who do so, but people are not going
to buy EVs without the charging infrastructure

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:11.779
to support it.

00:16:11.779 --> 00:16:16.970
EVs are comparable in cost to internal combustion
cars, their range is about what consumers

00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:21.810
demand, but what’s lagging behind is that
charging infrastructure.

00:16:21.810 --> 00:16:25.190
This isn’t even an exclusively American
problem.

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:29.649
In Australia, one can’t drive from Perth
to Sydney—the country’s forth and first

00:16:29.649 --> 00:16:35.480
most populous cities—in an EV, due to a
massive charging gap, while in Russia, despite

00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:41.410
similar incentives for EV purchases, there
are a total of 24 DC-fast chargers in the

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:43.800
entire country.

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:48.490
Of course some will always debate whether
governments should be incentivizing electric

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:53.860
vehicles at all, but regardless of that, they
are—it’s tough to find a developed country

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:58.960
that does not have some tax or other monetary
incentive for EV ownership.

00:16:58.960 --> 00:17:02.350
The point is that they’re incentivizing
the wrong way.

00:17:02.350 --> 00:17:10.140
EVs are very, very close to reaching the tipping-point
criteria for everything but charging.

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:14.790
Cost is not standing in the way, technology
is not standing in the way, infrastructure

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:18.780
is, so governments are putting the cart before
the horse.

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:23.520
Individual companies cannot reach the required
scale, and even if they did, as the format

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:28.950
war in the US proves, it probably wouldn’t
be the kind of scale that the mass-market

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:30.730
consumer demands.

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:35.270
Individual car companies can deal with making
individual electric vehicles attractive to

00:17:35.270 --> 00:17:40.239
consumers, the government doesn’t need to
worry about that, but infrastructure—that’s

00:17:40.239 --> 00:17:42.620
the government’s job.

00:17:42.620 --> 00:17:49.620
Governments run or regulate roads, and bridges,
and tunnels, and sidewalks, railways, airports,

00:17:49.620 --> 00:17:55.270
electric grids, dams, sewers, water supply
networks, and even fuel supply systems, because

00:17:55.270 --> 00:18:02.710
they are infrastructure, and infrastructure
is essential, so the only question is: why

00:18:02.710 --> 00:18:04.820
not charging?

00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:10.179
So, as you might have guessed by now, I own
an electric vehicle, so I took it to my local

00:18:10.179 --> 00:18:15.431
Tesla Supercharger to make a companion video
to this where I give a super detailed, super

00:18:15.431 --> 00:18:21.100
nerdy explanation of exactly how a Supercharger
works from a technical perspective.

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:26.620
You can find that companion video exclusively
on Nebula which, as you probably know by now,

00:18:26.620 --> 00:18:32.850
is home to tons of exclusive, ad-free content
from tons of your favorite educational creators.

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:37.750
The reason we can put companion videos like
this there is because of the way Nebula works—it

00:18:37.750 --> 00:18:41.330
doesn’t have an algorithm to punish us when
we make something different from our normal

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:45.940
stuff, and the direct subscriptions from users
help fund both these and other projects—like

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:47.630
our many Nebula originals.

00:18:47.630 --> 00:18:52.590
If you’re interested in watching all this
exclusive content, plus supporting loads of

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:58.210
independent, educational creators, you can
sign up using the CuriosityStream/Nebula bundle.

00:18:58.210 --> 00:19:03.990
Basically, you go to CuriosityStream.com/Wendover,
sign up for any subscription—but I suggest

00:19:03.990 --> 00:19:08.090
the yearly one since it’s on sale for less
than $15 a year right now—and then you have

00:19:08.090 --> 00:19:11.050
access to both streaming sites.

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:15.340
CuriosityStream itself has great stuff too
from more established names like Richard Hammond,

00:19:15.340 --> 00:19:19.500
of Top Gear and the Grand Tour, who made a
series about how weather actually works by

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:21.990
going out into extreme weather.

00:19:21.990 --> 00:19:26.740
All told, CuriosityStream is great thanks
to their seemingly endless library of top-quality

00:19:26.740 --> 00:19:32.050
stuff, and Nebula is great thanks to its exclusive,
early, and ad-free videos from the educational

00:19:32.050 --> 00:19:34.880
creators you already know and love.

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:39.350
CuriosityStream and Nebula together is greater
than great, though, because it’s only $15

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:42.710
a year with the current sale at CuriosityStream.com/Wendover.

